menu "libtracer"

    # Per-transport module selection (#393) — mirrors the core CMake options
    # LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_{UDP,TCP,WS,CAN}. All default y, so a stock node compiles
    # the full transport set (byte-identical to before this knob existed). Turn one
    # OFF to shed that transport's translation units from libtracer.a for a node that
    # never creates a connection of that kind — the RAM-audit "compose the C6 for its
    # role" lever (shed unused transports), with NO in-source feature macros: which
    # transport compiles is decided by which TUs the build selects. A SPEC of a
    # trimmed `kind` then resolves to SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND (the factory is simply absent).

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_UDP
        bool "Build the UDP datagram transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `udp` transport (udp_transport_t + transport_udp.cpp
            + its factory glue). ON by default. Turn OFF on a node that never creates
            a `kind=udp` connection to drop those TUs from the archive.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_TCP
        bool "Build the TCP stream transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `tcp` transport (tcp_transport_t / transport_tcp_server
            + transport_tcp.cpp + its factory glue). ON by default. Turn OFF on a node
            that never creates a `kind=tcp` connection.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        bool "Build the WebSocket transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `ws` transport (transport_ws.cpp + its factory glue,
            and the esp_http_server-backed httpd_ws_link_t on chip targets). ON by
            default. Turn OFF on a node that neither serves nor dials a WebSocket link.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_CAN
        bool "Build the CAN transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the CAN transport (transport_can.cpp portable framing/reassembly +
            the TWAI-backed can_link_t on chip targets, SocketCAN on the linux host).
            ON by default. Turn OFF on a node with no CAN bus — this also drops the
            TWAI driver glue (twai_link.cpp).

    config LIBTRACER_VERTEX_LOCK_STRIPES
        int "Vertex lock-stripe count"
        range 1 256
        default 16
        help
            Number of process-wide mutex/condvar lock stripes shared by every
            graph vertex (libtracer #361 section 2). This is the ONLY global
            mutable buffer libtracer links into a node: N stripes cost N lazily
            allocated FreeRTOS mutexes (~90 B of heap each) plus N condition
            variables, paid once a graph_t exists — independent of how many
            vertices you register.

            The stripe a vertex rides is chosen by hashing its pinned address,
            so lowering the count only raises CONTROL-PLANE lock contention
            (ring trim, edge/ACL mutation, await wake); the lock-free LKV
            read/write hot path is unaffected. On a single-core chip target a
            small count (4-8) reclaims RAM at negligible contention cost; the
            host/multi-core default stays 16.

            The core header (<libtracer/vertex.hpp>) already honors a
            -DLIBTRACER_VERTEX_LOCK_STRIPES override; this option is the ESP-IDF
            front door for it. The component propagates the value as a PUBLIC
            compile definition so every translation unit that includes
            <libtracer/vertex.hpp> agrees: the stripe table is an
            `inline constinit` object, and a size mismatch across TUs is an ODR
            violation.

endmenu
